WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.233
The compounding habits.

2
00:00:02.533 --> 00:00:05.600
None of these need permission, a title, or a budget.

3
00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:08.867
The gap is repetition, not talent.

4
00:00:09.167 --> 00:00:12.667
Over two or three years, small habits separate people sharply.

5
00:00:13.867 --> 00:00:15.567
Here are the habits, as a loop.

6
00:00:15.867 --> 00:00:16.825
Write in public.

7
00:00:16.825 --> 00:00:20.178
A monthly post on what you built and what surprised you.

8
00:00:20.178 --> 00:00:22.333
It is the highest return habit here.

9
00:00:22.633 --> 00:00:23.632
Reproduce results.

10
00:00:23.632 --> 00:00:28.733
You'll be right often enough to gain confidence, and wrong often enough to build skepticism.

11
00:00:29.033 --> 00:00:31.750
Keep a numbers notebook of every benchmark.

12
00:00:31.750 --> 00:00:34.467
In two years you estimate systems out loud.

13
00:00:34.767 --> 00:00:38.100
Teach one person, and find where your understanding is thin.

14
00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:42.500
Post-mortem every failure into a written lesson and a new eval case.

15
00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:46.700
And always keep one project running that you are not sure you can finish.

16
00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:48.800
Now your edge.

17
00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:51.826
Most engineers can't scope a business problem.

18
00:00:51.826 --> 00:00:54.433
Most architects can't ship the async Python.

19
00:00:54.733 --> 00:00:56.316
One ships without the case.

20
00:00:56.316 --> 00:00:58.367
The other designs and never builds.

21
00:00:58.667 --> 00:00:59.372
You do both.

22
00:00:59.372 --> 00:01:01.900
That combination is rarer than either half.

23
00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:04.367
Here is the timeline.

24
00:01:04.667 --> 00:01:11.467
In ninety days: three from-scratch builds, twelve papers, three write-ups, and one repo with evals and traces.

25
00:01:11.767 --> 00:01:18.533
In a year: all ten builds, forty plus papers, monthly writing, and one real open source contribution.

26
00:01:18.833 --> 00:01:22.199
In three years, people cite your work or use your tool.

27
00:01:22.199 --> 00:01:26.300
That is level four territory, reachable only by building in public.

28
00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:29.200
Here is one habit in action.

29
00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:34.000
Something fails: an outage, a hallucination, or a blown cost estimate.

30
00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:36.100
You write down what happened and why.

31
00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:39.967
Then it becomes a new eval case, so the system learns from failure.

32
00:01:41.167 --> 00:01:41.904
Let's recap.

33
00:01:41.904 --> 00:01:43.933
Write in public, the best return.

34
00:01:44.233 --> 00:01:46.800
Reproduce, measure, teach, and post-mortem.

35
00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:49.200
Always build one thing beyond your level.

36
00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:51.485
And be the architect who codes.

37
00:01:51.485 --> 00:01:53.533
Next up: the analytical toolkit.
